[ChapterName "Czech: The Old Indian"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/nRiq9SZx/zRxWIvzy"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/WatsonChenney"]
[UTCDate "2021.02.23"]
[UTCTime "21:23:06"]

1. d4 c6 2. c4 d6 3. Nc3 Nf6 { Black always plays the same set-up (except in rare cases). } 4. e4 g6 { The move I recommend here, although there are many other moves. Black wants to go for a typical King's Indian set-up, break in the center with e5, and wait to see what White wants. Admittedly, this isn't the most ambitious set-up, but it's still very playable. } 5. Nf3 Bg7 6. Be2 (6. g3 O-O 7. Bg2 e5 8. O-O Nbd7 9. h3 { Another variation of the King's Indian Defense. }) 6... O-O 7. O-O e5 { And we've completed our journey from Czech into the King's Indian territory. My goal isn't to deal with the specific middlegame plans and ideas that are possible, just to give you a feel for this versatile system that is the Czech/Pribyl System. } *


