1. c4 Nf6 { Preference, to stop the immediate e4. } (1... c6 2. e4 { [%csl Rg8,Rf6,Ge4,Re5][%cal Ge4e5,Re5f6] } 2... d6 { Is also fine. }) 2. Nc3 c6 3. g3 d6 { [%csl Bd6,Bc6,Bf6] } 4. Bg2 g6 5. Nf3 Bg7 6. O-O O-O { And again, we have a flexible set-up. Our goal isn't going to be traditional plans of preventing e4 or d4 and occupying the center. We are going to develop our pieces, and perhaps go for an attack on the queenside, trade pieces to minimize White's attack, and chip away at the center at the right moment. } *


[Event "✨Pirc/Czech System for Black: Lifetime Repertoire⁉️✨ 👀 🦾💖: Bread and Butter: The Main ideas against e4 🍞"]
[Date "2021.02.23"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B07"]
[Opening "Czech Defense"]
[StudyName "✨Pirc/Czech System for Black: Lifetime Repertoire⁉️✨ 👀 🦾💖"]
[ChapterName "Bread and Butter: The Main ideas against e4 🍞"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/nRiq9SZx/kqHpIvry"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/WatsonChenney"]
[UTCDate "2021.02.23"]
[UTCTime "21:49:16"]

1. e4 { Now your personal taste will decide if you play c6 (as if you were going to play the Caro-Kann) or d6, which would be the Pirc. } { [%csl Bc7,Bc6,Yd7,Yd6][%cal Bc7c6,Yd7d6] } 1... d6 (1... c6) 2. d4 Nf6 { Forcing White to defend the pawn and not continue aggressively occupying the center with pawns. } { [%csl Bf6,Re4][%cal Yf6e4] } 3. Nc3 c6 { Now we exit the Pirc highway for the Czech road. } { [%csl Yf2,Rf4][%cal Yf2f4] } 4. Nf3 { 4.f4 will be covered in a later chapter. } 4... Qa5 $140 { Of course Bg4 or g6 are also good, but I quite like this move. } { [%csl Re4] } 5. Bd3 { The main move, Bd2 and e5! being the other popular moves. Check the alternate lines for notes on how to meet each of these. } { [%csl Gd3,Ge4][%cal Gd3e4] } (5. Bd2 { Aggressively developing and x-raying the queen. } { [%csl Gd2,Ga5,Rc3,Rc7,Rf6][%cal Rd2a5,Rc3d5,Rc3b5] } 5... Nbd7 { Nbd7 is preferred. } (5... e5 6. dxe5 dxe5 7. Bc4 { And you move the Queen somewhere } 7... Qc7) 6. Nd5 Qd8 7. Nxf6+ Nxf6 8. Bd3 g6 { This line hasn't been played much, and although White has an edge, Black seems to do well from here. }) (5. e5! { Strong move, but I doubt most of your opponents will play this. } 5... Ne4! { If White now brings the bishop to d2, Black should capture it. } { [%csl Be4,Gd6,Rc3,Bd2][%cal Ge4d6,Ge4c3,Ge4d2] }) 5... Bg4 (5... e5 { A move that also make sense here. Black will bring the bishop to e7 and castle, and perhaps have a similar position to the Bg4 line if White takes on e5. } { [%csl Gf8,Ge8,Bg8,Gc1,Be3,Ge1,Bg1,Bd1,Be2][%cal Bf8e7,Be8g8,Bc1e3,Be1g1,Bd1e2] }) 6. h3 Bxf3 { Black shouldn't be afraid to give up the Bishop here, a trade one would normally avoid. 1. Black's position is a bit cramped, so it's best to trade pieces. 2. The Nf3 is a key defender of the center. } 7. Qxf3 Nbd7 *


