{ [%csl Bf2,Bg1,Bd2,Bg2,Yg4,Yf4,Ye4,Be2,Yd4,Yc4,Bc2,Bb2,Yb4,Gf3][%cal Yg2g4,Yf2f4,Ye2e4,Yd2d4,Yc2c4,Yb2b4,Gg1f3] }
1. e4 (1. Nf3 d6 2. g3 c6 3. Bg2 Nf6 4. O-O g6 5. d3 Bg7 6. Nbd2 O-O 7. e4 (7. c3 e5) 7... Nbd7 8. Re1 e5 $10 { With an equal game against the King's Indian Attack. https://lichess.org/1P2of78A/black }) (1. c4 c6 2. Nf3 d6 3. g3 Nf6 4. Bg2 g6 5. b3 Bg7 6. Bb2 O-O 7. O-O $10 Na6!? { and Black went on to win https://lichess.org/y1MKF2Cf/black }) (1. b4 c6 { This is actually a valid line against the Polish. } 2. Bb2 d6 (2... a5!) 3. c4 Nf6 $10) (1. g4 c6 2. Bg2 d6 3. h3 Nf6 { Just because we can! } (3... h5!? { The supposed "refutation" -- as if there needed to be one for the 𝙘𝙤𝙖𝙧𝙨𝙚, 𝙧𝙤𝙪𝙜𝙝 𝙪𝙣𝙘𝙤𝙪𝙩𝙝, 𝙧𝙪𝙙𝙚, 𝙘𝙧𝙪𝙙𝙚 Grob. But yeah, no hard feelings or anything. } 4. g5 e5)) (1. d4 c6 2. Bf4 d6 3. Nf3 Nf6 { With multiple ideas: 1. To win the bishop pair if allowed 2. To develop the Queen and attack the b2 pawn. 3. To play g6 and fianchetto the Bishop } 4. h3 (4. c3 h6 5. h3 g5!? 6. Bh2 Bf5) 4... h6 5. e3 g5! { Because, yes! }) (1. f4 { Ah, the Bird. 🐦 If you're too chicken 🐥 to play the From Gambit here, you can play the Czech/Pribyl System and possible transpose to the 𝒉𝒂𝒓𝒎𝒐𝒏𝒊𝒐𝒖𝒔 Rat Defense that we saw before. } 1... c6 (1... e5 2. fxe5 d6 { [%csl Ge5,Gf8,Rd6,Bg1,Gf3][%cal Bf8d6,Ye5d6,Yg1f3] }) 2. Nf3 d6 3. e4 Nf6) 1... d6 2. d4 (2. f4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 { https://lichess.org/j7feFCBM/black a hideous example. }) 2... Nf6 3. Nc3 c6 4. Nf3 (4. Bc4?! Qa5!? (4... d5!) 5. Bd2 Qb6!? { Being greedy is fine, whatever the engine says. } { [%csl Rb2,Rd4] }) 4... Qa5 5. e5 (5. Bd3 Bg4 6. Bd2 Qb6! { Putting the pressure to White. This scores well for Black against lower rated opponents and nice for players who like sharp tactical lines. } 7. Na4 Qc7 8. c4 e5) 5... Ne4 6. Bd2?! Nxd2 7. Qxd2 dxe5 8. dxe5 Bg4 $10 { https://lichess.org/3jR3vjut/black } *


