1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 c6 { Nothing wrong with this move, although e5 (or even g6) is by far more popular. The main drawback with c6 is that it allows f4. But with c6 we are trying to steer the game away from typical Pirc lines. } (3... e5 4. Nf3 (4. dxe5 { This is is a forcing line, so it's best to be prepared for it. } 4... dxe5 5. Qxd8+ Kxd8 { [%csl Gf1,Yc4,Rf7,Gc1,Yg5,Bf6][%cal Yf1c4,Yc1g5] } 6. Bg5 Be6 7. O-O-O+ Nd7 8. f4 exf4) 4... Nbd7 5. Bc4 Be7 6. O-O h6 7. h3 c6 8. a4 Qc7 9. Be3 Nf8 10. Qd2 g5 11. Rad1 Ng6 { This is our master plan, but how to find the right move order 🥺 }) 4. Nf3 (4. f4 Qa5 { This probably best. }) 4... Qc7 { The start of our alternative plan. } 5. Bd3 { The most common move at lower levels, although it's hard to say as this is already an infrequently occurring position. } (5. Bc4 b5!) (5. e5 Nd5 6. Nxd5 cxd5 7. Bf4 e6 8. Bd3 Nc6 { Perhaps the most critical line, but not too often played. }) 5... Nbd7 (5... e5 6. O-O (6. dxe5 dxe5 $10) 6... Nbd7 { alternative }) 6. O-O e5 7. h3 { [%csl Gh3,Rg4,Bf6,Be3,Gc1][%cal Yf6g4,Yc1e3] } 7... Be7 (7... g6 { also good }) 8. Be3 Nf8!? 9. Ne2 h6 (9... Ng6 10. c4 (10. c3 Nh5 11. Qd2) 10... Nh5 { [%csl Ge2,Rg3,Bg6,Bh5][%cal Re2g3,Yg6f4,Yh5f4] } 11. b4 O-O 12. Rc1 Bd7 { This 9...Ng6 line is more complicated, but also more novel and thus useful. One thing to remember is that we don't play Ng5 to play f5? any time soon, because of exf and g4 tactics. }) 10. c4 { [%csl Gg7,Bg5][%cal Yg7g5] } 10... g5 11. Rc1 { [%csl Bg5,Rg4][%cal Yg5g4] } *


