1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 Nbd7?! { Typical GingerGM poison! ☠️ } 4. Nf3 { Principled, but I think f4! just loads up the pressure on Black. } (4. f4! { You know it's bad when you have to have an Anti-Philidor. But like before, we'll look at the Nf3 lines as well, which might give us some more hope... or heartaches. 🤨 } 4... e5 5. Nf3 c6 6. dxe5 (6. Bd3?! Qc7 7. O-O Be7 { Dream on, this won't ever happen. There are far too many juicy options for White to allow us to get our beloved setup. }) 6... dxe5 7. fxe5 (7. Bc4 Qa5 8. Ng5 Bb4 9. Bxf7+) 7... Ng4 8. e6 fxe6 9. Ng5! { Did I mention I'm not a fan? 😫 }) 4... c6?! (4... e5 5. Bc4 (5. dxe5 dxe5 6. Bc4 $10) 5... h6! { [%csl Bf3,Gg5,Rf7,Bc4][%cal Yf3g5,Yg5f7,Yc4f7] }) 5. e5! { hAHa! No, you're not getting away with this, but looking at the stats from online play you just must might if they don't play this move. } 5... Nd5 6. Nxd5 cxd5 7. e6 fxe6 8. Ng5 Qa5+ { The computer seem to prefer Nf6 (at least at low depth), and at first sight it seems to hold onto the pawn, which is why it's played online. Unfortunately it invites some severe headaches. } (8... Nf6 9. Bb5+ Bd7 10. Nxe6 Qa5+?? (10... Qc8 11. Bxd7+ $14) 11. Bd2 { Ooops!! } 11... Qb6 (11... Qxb5?? $18 12. Nc7+ { [%csl Re8,Ra8,Rb5][%cal Bc7e8,Bc7a8,Bc7b5] }) 12. a4) 9. Bd2 Qb6 10. Nxe6 (10. Bd3 Nf6 11. Bxh7!? Nxh7 12. Qh5+ Kd7 13. Nxh7 Qxb2 14. O-O Qxd4 15. Be3 Qe5 16. Nf6+ Qxf6 17. Qxh8 $13) 10... Nf6 11. Nxf8 (11. Bb5+ Kf7 12. Ng5+ Kg8) 11... Rxf8 *


